{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42472 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "N-Methyltyramine is implicated in the target pathway leading to DNA polymerase iota through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that N-Methyltyramine acts as a target for Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9, which in turn is targeted by multiple compounds, including Dendridine A and a related fatty acid derivative. These targets ultimately converge on DNA polymerase iota, indicating a potential mechanism where N-Methyltyramine may influence the activity of these intermediaries, which then modulate DNA polymerase iota. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for N-Methyltyramine in regulating DNA polymerase iota through a chain of enzymatic interactions, possibly involving redox or metabolic processes. The evidence supports a mechanism where N-Methyltyramine could either directly or indirectly influence DNA polymerase iota, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated.",
